 ],
 "literal": "And tap the toddy! And slaughter the ram! / Under the canopy of slender posts erected on the fresh ground, / spread abundantly fine sand brought by the river water! / Having destroyed the vanguard of the enemies, standing at the rear / to my lord who is coming with the herd of cattle, / his companions are indeed of greater grace than himself!",
 "translation": "Tap the toddy! Slaughter the ram! Under the canopy of slender posts erected on the fresh ground, spread abundantly the fine sand brought by the river! To my lord who is returning with the captured herd of cattle, after destroying the enemies' vanguard and guarding the rear, his companions are indeed of even greater grace than himself!",
 "modern": "கள்ளை அவிழ்த்து வையுங்கள்! கிடாயை வெட்டுங்கள்! புதிய நிலத்தில் அமைக்கப்பட்ட மெல்லிய கால்களை உடைய பந்தலின் கீழ், ஆற்று நீர் கொண்டு வந்த இளமணலை நிறையப் பரப்புங்கள்! பகைவர்களின் முன்னணியை அழித்து, பின்னே நின்று காத்து, கைப்பற்றிய ஆநிரையோடு வரும் என் தலைவனுக்கு, அவனோடு இருக்கும் தோழர்கள் அவனை விடவும் மிகுந்த மென்மைத் தன்மை உடையவர்கள் ஆவர்!",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Veṭci (cattle-raiding) tiṇai and the Uṇṭāṭṭu (festive drinking) theme. The speaker is a resident of the village preparing a grand feast to welcome the victorious warriors returning with the captured cattle. The speaker instructs the villagers to open the toddy jars, slaughter the sacrificial rams, and prepare a comfortable canopy with fresh river sand. The hero is praised for his bravery in destroying the enemy's vanguard and then guarding the rear of the retreating herd. Crucially, the poem highlights the nobility of his companions, stating that they are even more gracious and gentle than the hero himself, reflecting the deep bond of comradeship in heroic warfare. Key words include 'naṟavu' (toddy), 'nirai' (cattle herd), and 'peruñcāyalar' (those of great grace).",
